=== agent-deck connect: Any assistant (it sets itself up) (name "assistant", scopes read) ===
Token file: /home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/assistant.token (0600). Revoke: agent-deck connect revoke assistant

--- 1. Config: the assistant picks: its MCP server config if it has one, otherwise the HTTP API ---
Assistant: follow https://raw.githubusercontent.com/asheshgoplani/agent-deck/main/docs/assistants/SETUP-PROMPT.md with this connection; use the first option you support.

A) MCP over stdio (you run on this machine; the token stays in its file):
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "agentdeck": {
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/agent-deck",
      "args": [
        "connect",
        "serve",
        "--token-file",
        "/home/me/.local/share/agent-deck/assistants/assistant.token"
      ]
    }
  }
}

B) MCP over HTTP (needs `agent-deck web` running; cloud assistants need a public HTTPS address):
URL: http://127.0.0.1:8420/mcp
Header: Authorization: Bearer adk_GOLDENTOKEN

C) Plain HTTP API (same token, JSON in and out):
curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer adk_GOLDENTOKEN" "http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions"
curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer adk_GOLDENTOKEN" "http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions/SESSION_ID/output"
curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer adk_GOLDENTOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"message":"..."}' "http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1/sessions/SESSION_ID/send"   # needs scope send

--- 2. Test ---
agent-deck connect test --name assistant

--- 3. Guide: keep this text in your assistant's memory, skills or standing instructions ---
You are connected to agent-deck on my machine as "assistant" with scopes: read.
agent-deck is a terminal session manager for AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, OpenCode and others): each session is one agent working in its own terminal. Through the agent-deck tools you can see those sessions and, when allowed, drive them.

Tools (MCP server "agentdeck"; the HTTP API at http://127.0.0.1:8420/api/assistant/v1 mirrors them):
- whoami: your name, scopes and limits
- list_sessions {group?, status?}: all sessions with id, title, tool, status, group, path (read)
- session_status {session}: one session's details (read)
- read_output {session}: the last answer of that session's agent (read)
- send_message {session, message}: type a message into a session (send)
- approve {session, choice?}: answer a visible approval prompt (approve)
- launch_session {path, title, tool?, message?}: start a new session (launch)

Rules:
1. Call list_sessions first and refer to sessions by id.
2. Read before acting: check session_status or read_output before you send or approve anything.
3. ASK THE USER and wait for a clear yes before send_message, approve or launch_session; show the exact text you will send.
4. Never stop, delete, restart or kill sessions. There is no tool for it; do not use a shell or any other way to do it.
5. Never read, print or change credentials, tokens or agent-deck's own files (~/.agent-deck, ~/.local/share/agent-deck).
6. If a tool answers "scope missing", do not work around it: tell the user the exact command from the answer (for example: agent-deck connect any --name assistant --scopes read,send).
7. Keep reports short: one line per session (title, status, what it is waiting for).

To allow more, run: agent-deck connect any --name assistant --scopes read,send,approve,launch (pick only the scopes you need; this rotates the token, stdio configs pick the new one up from the token file, HTTP configs need the new token)
=== end ===
